<section>
  <h2>HTML & Web Programming FAQ</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>What is semantic HTML?</summary>
    <p>Semantic HTML uses elements that clearly describe their meaning and role, such as <header>, <article>, and <footer>, making pages more accessible and easier to maintain.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How do I link CSS to an HTML page?</summary>
    <p>You can link a CSS file using the <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tag inside the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> section, for example: <code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"&gt;</code>.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is the difference between inline, internal, and external CSS?</summary>
    <p><strong>Inline CSS</strong> is written directly in the element’s style attribute, <em>internal CSS</em> is placed inside a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block in the HTML file, and <strong>external CSS</strong> is linked from a separate stylesheet file.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Why is accessibility important in web development?</summary>
    <p>Accessibility ensures that websites can be used by people with disabilities. Using semantic HTML, proper alt text, and ARIA roles helps create inclusive experiences for all users.</p>
  </details>
</section>
